From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:53:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: mediatek: add an MT6797 SPM mem suspend experiment

Route A stage S1 of the cpuidle record: register "mem" suspend
operations that run the vendor SPM suspend program on CPU0, with the
CPU kept powered in WFI (SPM_FLAG_DIS_CPU_PDN) and a PCM-timer wake,
so the project can measure what the vendor program delivers.

The program is not part of the driver. At load it reads
mediatek/mt6797/pcm_suspend.bin, the owner's own vendor file, checks
its SHA-256, copies the code into the spm-reserve-memory carveout and
takes the sixteen event vectors from the file's descriptor.

.prepare sets the MT6351 sleep-side bits (VGPU low-IQ, power-good
force, VCORE 0.7 V sleep voltage) through the PMIC wrapper's regmap.
.enter checks that CPU0 is alone, the PCM is idle and the modem, C2K
and CONNSYS are off, then loads the PMIC wrapper's suspend-phase
commands, initialises and kicks the PCM, sets ACINACTM on the three
clusters and waits in WFI for the PCM-return interrupt (SPM SPI 180,
mapped through sysirq and enabled for wake only during suspend). It
restores every SPM, wrapper and MCUCFG word it changed and leaves the
PCM in reset, as found. No PSCI or SiP call is made.

The flags set SPM_FLAG_DIS_VPROC_VSRAM_DVS as well, so the program does
not drive the A72 buck over I2C6. PASR_DPD_3 is zeroed for the sleep so
the program's timer-wake loop returns on the first timer. The GCE,
AUXADC and I2C_APPM gates the program flips are put back as found, and
CLK_CFG_0 and the three gate words are logged before and after.

Local experiment, not for upstream; it depends on a non-free program
the project does not ship.
